sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch13-19> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Because the embryo is the animal in a less modified state that partly reveals the structure of its progenitor, two groups that pass through the same or similar embryonic stages are descended from the same or similar parents, however different their adults; this is why embryonic characters are the most valuable for classification.";
    dc:type "historical-inference";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Community of embryonic structure reveals community of descent";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Charles Darwin, On the Origin of Species (1st ed., London: John Murray, 1859), Chapter XIII, “Mutual Affinities of Organic Beings: Morphology: Embryology: Rudimentary Organs”; section “EMBRYOLOGY, laws of, explained by variations not supervening at an early age, and being inherited at a corresponding age”. Scope: animal groups, e.g. cirripedes recognised as crustaceans by their larvae"
}
